# The node detail's position section, in both the shapes it comes in.
#
# The two rows this scene exists for are the ones a fix can overstate. A precision is what the
# sender rounded its coordinates to, and reading it as "16 bits" told a user nothing they could
# act on; a fix age is the node's clock when the node dated it and ours when it did not, and
# saying "Fix" over our own arrival time claimed the node's account of itself.
#
# Foxtrot Mobile is a handheld with a live GPS: it dates its own fix and rounds nothing off.
# Alfa Ridge is a repeater with a channel precision set, and - like most nodes on a real mesh -
# leaves the timestamp off the air, so its section answers with our clock and says so.

scene demo
tab nodes

# Row 0 of the Nodes list is the Map, so a node's row is its index in the demo roster plus one:
# Foxtrot Mobile is seeds[6] and row 7, Alfa Ridge is seeds[1] and row 2.

# Foxtrot Mobile: satellites, and a fix the node dated itself. Its detail has no Precision row,
# so Fix is one row earlier than Alfa's, which is the difference this scene is about.
key down 7
key a
key down 33
hold 1500
key b

# Alfa Ridge: a rounded position, aged by when we heard it. B left the cursor on Foxtrot's own
# row, so this walks back up the list rather than indexing into it.
key up 5
key a
key down 34
hold 1500
key b
